# Base Cover Letter — Senior Software Engineer roles

> Reusable master letter. Pair it with a JD and tailor only the marked spots, rather than rebuilding from scratch.
> Distilled 2026-06-15 from the Virgin, Wesfarmers, Jora/SEEK, and Canva letters + the captured cover-letter voice rules.
> Source of truth is this `.md`. Export to `.docx` at submission time (same boundary as the resumes).

## How to use it (90 seconds per role)

1. Fill the two header fields: `[ROLE TITLE]` and `[COMPANY]`.
2. Rewrite only the **why-company paragraph** `[TAILOR]` — one genuine reason the company/role matters, grounded in what they actually do. This is the paragraph that earns the letter.
3. Optional **JD bridge**: if the JD leans hard on one thing (a language, a domain, a platform), add one honest sentence connecting your work to it. Do not add a stack checklist — the resume carries the keywords.
4. Optional **emphasis toggle**: for an AI-forward role keep the thesis as-is; for a pure-backend role, swap the opening thesis for the backend-led variant at the bottom of this file.
5. Optional **referral**: drop the referral line into the why-company paragraph only (never the opener).
6. Tense: ANZ, Macquarie, Leidos are past ("I was", "I architected"). AccessBuddy and LIMSOC are present ("I act as", "I run").
7. Before the PDF goes out: cross-check JD keyword coverage at the application level (resume + letter together), and pdftotext-verify the export for the ti/tf ligature bug.
8. Banned phrasing: never use the "you are describing the work I already do" construction or its cousins ("this is the work I have been doing", "is exactly the work I have been doing", "is the work I have done for years", "the role you describe is what I already do"). Nick dislikes it. State the capability directly instead.
9. **AccessBuddy is supplementary only — never lead a paragraph with it.** Every paragraph must lead with paid or professional proof (ANZ, Macquarie, Leidos, or the LIMSOC national strategy). AccessBuddy may appear at most as a brief closing clause ("Outside paid work I keep building, most recently AccessBuddy, a multi-tenant SaaS I shipped solo"). Never "commercial" or "paying", never the first concrete proof in a paragraph, never in the opener. See `career-pivot.md` settled decisions.
10. **Mentoring and leadership are first-class proof.** When the JD asks for leading or mentoring engineers (most senior roles do), make it explicit, not implied. Concrete receipts: led a four-engineer team at Macquarie through design and delivery; scaled a delivery practice from two teams to six at Leidos, mentoring engineers into the standards set and lifting team satisfaction by 80 per cent. Do not rely on "lifting the people around me" alone to carry it.

